// `ntime` — the UMBRELLA. The public surface of the library, in one file.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. `use` is not transitive in this language
// (the compiler's `MODULE_REFERENCE.md` §2.3: symbols imported into a module
// are NOT automatically re-exported, and `pub use` is the only way to
// re-export). A consumer that imported `src/cal/cal.npk` would therefore get
// `cal`'s names and nothing else, and a consumer wanting the library would
// write six imports. So the library re-exports deliberately, and the cost of
// doing it by hand is the FEATURE: the public surface is a list a reviewer can
// read in one sitting (0.0.1 P-8, `BUILD.md` B-16).
//
// THE RULE FOR THIS FILE, AND IT IS THE WHOLE RULE:
//
//     ONE LINE PER PUBLIC NAME. NEVER A `.*` GLOB.
//
// A glob would make the surface whatever the modules happen to declare, which
// is the opposite of a reviewed surface — and it would silently re-export
// `host`'s impure five, which is the one thing `SAFETY.md` §3 exists to keep
// deliberate. Adding a line here is the moment a name becomes public and, by
// TM-013, a thing a MAJOR version is needed to take away.
//
// IT WAS EMPTY UNTIL CYCLE 0.0.4 AND THAT WAS THE POINT; it re-exports 59
// names now [[sweep: lib_reexports=59]], below -- 57 until cycle 0.1.3c added
// `Pod` and `bytes_capacity`, 50 until cycle 0.1.3 added
// the seven derived fields, 48 until cycle 0.1.1 added `date_to_days` and
// `days_to_date`, counted by `grep -c` and
// read back from `check_denominators`' `measured:` line. (This paragraph
// still read "EMPTY TODAY ... this file re-exports nothing" two subcycles
// after they were added — C6. It then carried `35` through cycle 0.1.0's
// thirteen additions until the tag was added: an UNTAGGED derived number in a
// live document is history, which is TM-142's whole rule, and this file was
// carrying one at the moment it was quoting the rule. The tag was written `49`
// first, from counting the list in the head rather than with `grep -c`, and
// `check_denominators` is what said 48 — which is the mechanism working on
// the very number added to prove the mechanism works.)
// `tests/conformance/import.npk` compiled against the empty surface and goes
// on compiling as the surface grows. The day it does NOT, the surface changed
// incompatibly, which is exactly what that test is for.
//
// THE SHAPE THE LINES TAKE, so the first one added is not also the first one
// designed (the form is the compiler's own `lib/nbridge.npk`, and it is
// checked: `pub use "<relative path>".<name>;`):
//
//     pub use "./core/vec.npk".Vec;
//     pub use "./core/vec.npk".vec_init;
//     pub use "./cal/cal.npk".CivilDate;
//     pub use "./host/host.npk".host_now_utc;
//
// ORDER THEM BY LAYER, base first — core, cal, span, zone, fmt, host — which
// is `BUILD.md` B-17's diagram read left to right, so the file's shape and the
// dependency graph's shape are the same shape.
//
// `host` IS RE-EXPORTED FROM HERE AND FROM NOWHERE ELSE. It is the one module
// nothing in the library may import (B-17, TM-018); this file and an
// application are the two exceptions, and this comment is the reason a reader
// meets before `check_layering` enforces it at 0.0.3.
mod:lib;

// ===========================================================================
// core -- the storage primitives (cycle 0.0.4). THE FIRST PUBLIC NAMES.
// ===========================================================================
//
// ONE LINE PER PUBLIC NAME, NEVER A `.*` GLOB, and ordered by layer with the
// base first -- which is `BUILD.md` B-17's diagram read left to right, so this
// file's shape and the dependency graph's shape are the same shape.
//
// EVERY LINE HERE IS A COMMITMENT. By TM-013 a name on this list is a thing a
// MAJOR version is needed to take away, so adding one is the moment to ask
// whether it should be public at all -- which is the whole reason the umbrella
// is written by hand rather than globbed.

// `Vec<T>` -- FOR A NON-OWNING `T`. See `core/vec.npk`'s header, TM-132,
// TM-136 and TM-150. O-N17 -- the defect the restriction was first written
// against -- is FIXED at pin `aaffb87`, and O-N19 -- `NITPICK-TYPE-046` not
// firing inside a generic body, the reason it stood on from 0.0.5 -- is FIXED
// at `c3bdae2` by the compiler's D-264, which refuses a copy of a `T` place
// where it is written. The restriction stands on the one reason left: four of
// the nine operations owe an element drop at an owning `T` and perform none
// (`SAFETY.md` S-18d).
//
// SINCE CYCLE 0.1.0c (TM-156) its `items` is `hidden` and its `count` and `cap`
// are `sealed` under the prelude's `ListLen`: a consumer reads the two lengths
// and touches nothing else, and owes `LimitViolated` for the check on them.
//
// SINCE CYCLE 0.1.3c (TM-193, TM-194) A `Vec` IS MOVE-ONLY -- a copy of one is
// `NITPICK-TYPE-046`, and a transfer is `move(...)` -- and `vec_at` reads
// through a LOAN, bounded by `T: Pod`, so the element it hands back is a copy
// of a `T` that owns nothing and at an owning `T` the call is refused
// (`NITPICK-TYPE-017`). `Pod` is public so a consumer's own type that owns
// nothing can implement it, in one line beside its declaration.
pub use "./core/vec.npk".Vec;
pub use "./core/vec.npk".Pod;
pub use "./core/vec.npk".vec_init;
pub use "./core/vec.npk".vec_reserve;
pub use "./core/vec.npk".vec_push;
pub use "./core/vec.npk".vec_at;
pub use "./core/vec.npk".vec_set;
pub use "./core/vec.npk".vec_pop;
pub use "./core/vec.npk".vec_truncate;
pub use "./core/vec.npk".vec_clear;
pub use "./core/vec.npk".vec_free;

// `Bytes` -- the byte sink every formatter writes into. NO `bytes_free`, and
// its absence is the design (P-24, TM-133): the body is a managed `buffer` and
// its scope-exit drop IS the reclaim.
//
// `bytes_view` HANDS OUT A VIEW THAT GROWTH INVALIDATES (S-18e, TM-139), and
// both it and `bytes_push` are on this list. A view is valid until the next
// call that can grow the sink -- `push`, `extend`, `extend_str`, `put_uint`,
// `put_int`, `reserve` -- and reading it afterwards returns the allocator's
// poison, not the sink's bytes. Its header states the rule; this line is here
// because a public surface is where a consumer looks first.
//
// SINCE CYCLE 0.1.0c (TM-156) `body` and `len` are `sealed` and `len` carries
// `ListLen`. The seal keeps the header, NOT the bytes: a write THROUGH
// `b.body.ptr` still passes (D-313), which `bytes.npk`'s header measures and
// the workbench's question 9 carries to the author. *(Until cycle 0.1.3c.
// Since then `body` is `hidden` (TM-195), so a consumer cannot name it at all,
// and reads the sink's capacity through `bytes_capacity`.)*
pub use "./core/bytes.npk".Bytes;
pub use "./core/bytes.npk".bytes_init;
pub use "./core/bytes.npk".bytes_reserve;
pub use "./core/bytes.npk".bytes_push;
pub use "./core/bytes.npk".bytes_extend;
pub use "./core/bytes.npk".bytes_extend_str;
pub use "./core/bytes.npk".bytes_put_uint;
pub use "./core/bytes.npk".bytes_put_int;
pub use "./core/bytes.npk".bytes_len;
pub use "./core/bytes.npk".bytes_capacity;
pub use "./core/bytes.npk".bytes_view;
pub use "./core/bytes.npk".bytes_take;
pub use "./core/bytes.npk".bytes_clear;

// The named bounds. PUBLIC BECAUSE A CALLER CHECKING A RANGE BEFORE CALLING
// needs the same numbers the library checks against -- `SAFETY.md` S-12 makes
// every range stated and checked, and a caller that cannot read the bound has
// to duplicate it, which is the second copy `check_constants_named` exists to
// prevent.
pub use "./core/limits.npk".NTIME_PARSE_MAX;
pub use "./core/limits.npk".NTIME_PATH_MAX;
pub use "./core/limits.npk".NTIME_YEAR_MIN;
pub use "./core/limits.npk".NTIME_YEAR_MAX;
pub use "./core/limits.npk".NTIME_DAY_MIN;
pub use "./core/limits.npk".NTIME_DAY_MAX;
pub use "./core/limits.npk".NTIME_SECS_MIN;
pub use "./core/limits.npk".NTIME_SECS_MAX;
pub use "./core/limits.npk".NTIME_OFFSET_MIN;
pub use "./core/limits.npk".NTIME_OFFSET_MAX;
pub use "./core/limits.npk".NTIME_SECS_PER_DAY;
pub use "./core/limits.npk".NTIME_NANOS_PER_SEC;
pub use "./core/limits.npk".NTIME_DIGITS_MAX;

// ===========================================================================
// cal -- the civil calendar (cycle 0.1.0). THE FIRST ERROR IDENTITY.
// ===========================================================================
//
// `ETimeValue` IS THE FIRST OF THE THREE (TM-017, S-1) AND THE FIRST LINE HERE
// THAT COSTS A CONSUMER SOMETHING IT CANNOT DECLINE. REACH-002 makes it a
// mandatory `failsafe` arm in every program that imports `cal`, and taking it
// away again is a MAJOR version (TM-013, S-2). Measured at pin `aaffb87`: a
// program importing only `src/cal/cal.npk` owes NINE identities, not one --
// `cal.ETimeValue` plus the four floor arms plus `DivByZero`, `DivOverflow`,
// `IntOverflow` and `OutOfBounds`, which `cal`'s own `%`, `+` and table index
// arm in the CONSUMER however pure the consumer's code is (S-4b, S-4c, TM-107).
// At pin `c3bdae2` it is ELEVEN: the floor is six, `StackExhausted` and
// `MachineFault` having joined it (cycle 0.1.0b, TM-155), and nothing else
// moved. The number is read from `NITPICK-REACH-003`'s own list on every run by
// `check_failsafe_arms`, in both directions, and never predicted.
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".ETimeValue;

// The detail enum (S-3). PAYLOAD-FREE BECAUSE AN `error:` CANNOT CARRY ONE --
// measured, `tests/probe/probe14_error_payload_refused.npk`, and C-5b is the
// amendment. It is public because a caller that will one day be handed a
// `ValueFault` needs to be able to name the type; HOW a refusing constructor
// hands it back is O-X8 and is deliberately not settled here.
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".ValueFault;

// The civil types. Field order is comparison order (C-6) and the fields are
// NARROW, so every constructor below validates before it narrows -- there is
// no checked narrowing in this language (TM-105, S-15b).
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".CivilDate;
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".CivilTime;
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".CivilDateTime;
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".Weekday;
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".Month;

// THE VALIDATING CONSTRUCTORS, AND THERE IS NO UNCHECKED ONE IN THE MODULE
// (C-8). Read C-8b before relying on that sentence for more than it says: the
// language has no private field and no `opaque` struct with a body, so a
// CONSUMER can still write `CivilDate{ year: 32000i32, month: 99u8, day: 99u8 }`
// -- measured at pin `aaffb87`, it compiles, links and runs at exit 0. The
// guarantee is about every value THIS LIBRARY PRODUCES, and `check_civil_literal`
// is what keeps the library itself inside it.
//
// SINCE CYCLE 0.1.0c THAT PARAGRAPH IS HISTORY, and C-8c (TM-157) is the rule:
// every field of `CivilDate` and `CivilTime` is `sealed`, so the literal above
// is `NITPICK-TYPE-079` in every module but `cal` (measured at `c3bdae2`), the
// guarantee is the TYPE's again but for `wild` storage reinterpreted by `=>!`,
// and `check_civil_literal` is retired because the compiler now checks what it
// checked (TM-158).
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".civil_date;
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".civil_time;

// The leap rule and the month lengths. Here rather than at 0.1.1 because
// `civil_date` cannot refuse February 30th without them, and a constructor that
// validates three of its four conditions is not a validating constructor.
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".is_leap_year;
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".days_in_month;

// Hinnant's two algorithms (cycle 0.1.1, C-10, TM-016): the day number of a
// date, epoch 1970-01-01 = 0, and the date of a day number. `date_to_days` is
// `never fails` and total over every field value; `days_to_date` refuses a day
// outside `[NTIME_DAY_MIN, NTIME_DAY_MAX]` with `ETimeValue` before any
// arithmetic, and builds its answer through `civil_date` (TM-162).
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".date_to_days;
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".days_to_date;

// C-7: Monday = 1 ... Sunday = 7, with the C convention beside it because a
// caller porting code will look for it. The enum's own order stays ISO's.
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".weekday_number;
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".weekday_number_sunday_first;

// The derived fields (cycle 0.1.3; C-13, C-14, C-15): a date's weekday, its day
// of the year and its ISO week date -- computed from its day number, never
// stored -- and the two validating constructors that build a date back from an
// ordinal date and from an ISO week date, each refusing through its `Result`
// before any arithmetic on its arguments (S-12). SEVEN NAMES (TM-169), each a
// MAJOR version to take away (TM-013), and none carries `cal_`: `BUILD.md` B-15, as
// amended at cycle 0.1.3, says a public name is the one its specification gives
// it, and `CALENDAR.md` C-13 ... C-15 give these. They move the arm bill by
// nothing -- measured, eleven for `cal` and thirteen here, as before.
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".weekday;
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".day_of_year;
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".ordinal_to_date;
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".iso_week_year;
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".iso_week_number;
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".iso_weekday;
pub use "./cal/cal.npk".iso_week_to_date;
